A blank bot that simply goes online and prints logs can be written in just a few lines.

Here is everything you need to do:

### Step 1. Create a project and set up dependencies
Open the terminal in VS Code and create a new project:
```bash
cargo new my_discord_bot
cd my_discord_bot
```

Now open the Cargo.toml file and add the serenity library along with the tokio library for asynchrony. The [dependencies] section should look like this:

```toml
[dependencies]
serenity = "0.12"
tokio = { version = "1.0", features = ["full"] }
```

---

### Step 2. Bot Code (src/main.rs)
Clear the src/main.rs file and paste this ready-to-use code. It does exactly the same thing: starts up, authenticates, and silently stays online.

```rust
use serenity::prelude::*;
use serenity::model::gateway::Ready;
use serenity::async_trait;

// Create a structure to handle events from Discord
struct Handler;

#[async_trait]
impl EventHandler for Handler {
    // This event fires once when the bot successfully connects
    async fn ready(&self, _: Context, ready: Ready) {
        println!("Bot successfully started as: {}", ready.user.name);
        println!("The bot is just sitting online using Rust and doing nothing.");
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // Insert your bot token here
    let token = "INSERT_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE";

    // Configure Intents. Basic intents are enough for a blank bot
    let intents = GatewayIntents::GUILDS;

    // Create the bot client
    let mut client = Client::builder(&token, intents)
        .event_handler(Handler)
        .await
        .expect("Error creating client");

    // Start the bot. It will stay online indefinitely until you close the terminal
    if let Err(why) = client.start().await {
        println!("Bot error: {:?}", why);
    }
}
```

---

### Step 3. Launch
In the VS Code terminal, simply run the following command:
```bash
cargo run
```
Rust will automatically download the required libraries from the internet, compile the project, and launch the bot. In Rust, this happens entirely automatically — no CMake files or manual linker configurations are needed.
